{"objective":"Close the four hardening gaps left after the wave-5 envelope work, in the order the external evidence ranks them: release mechanics (a release that can be verified), freshness that acts (the documented failure mode for agent-facing knowledge), one source for the envelope field set (the structural countermeasure to measured AI-era duplication), and a transport corpus that makes the specification falsifiable. Evidence, not opinion: DORA 2025 reports AI adoption still correlates with delivery instability while ~30% of engineers distrust generated code; GitClear measures code-block duplication +81% and refactoring -70% across 623M changes (2023-2026); DataHub and Atlan document stale context as the dominant agent failure mode ('confidently wrong'); Thoughtworks names spec-driven development a 2025 key practice and locates its risk in spec/code drift, which is exactly what the exchange specification had.","scope":"1. Release mechanics: one verification pass per repository plus one cross-repository pass that asserts every version axis agrees (tag vs anvil.yaml, README cli axis vs anvil, go.mod pin vs released core tag, pack.Version vs anvil.yaml vs requiresEka, corpus version vs EKA vs versioning.md). Verification, not automation: bumping stays a deliberate human action, but a half-done release becomes detectable in one command. 2. Freshness: make the validity horizon actionable — list what is overdue and what is approaching, and give CI a flag that fails on overdue knowledge. 3. Envelope manifest: one table of field descriptors in conformance drives the JSON allowlist, the frontmatter keys, the shared validation pass, the unit projection and both renderers, so a field cannot be added to one side only. 4. Transport corpus: a versioned set of golden packages living with the specification, each exercising one case (full envelope, empty envelope, every refusal class, a comment unit), which the implementation asserts round-trips byte-identically. Exit criterion: the corpus fails if any adapter stops reading a field, the version axes are checkable in one command per repo plus one across repos, stale knowledge is listed and can fail a build, and the envelope field set exists once.","outOfScope":"Not here: spec-as-source code generation (beta tooling, and it enlarges batches), multi-agent orchestration (documented failure pattern), new MCP tools or artifact tokens, and any change to the conformance rules' severity model. Also out: automating the bump/push step — the failures observed were undetected drift, not slow typing."}